Use Case Title:
“Sentinel AML: AI-Powered Anti-Money Laundering & Financial Crime Investigation Platform”
Problem Statement:
Financial institutions process millions of transactions every day. Traditional Anti-Money Laundering (AML) systems primarily rely on static, rule-based checks (for example, transactions above a fixed threshold), which generate a large number of false positives and fail to detect sophisticated laundering techniques.
Modern financial criminals use complex methods such as:
Structuring (Smurfing): Splitting large amounts into multiple small transactions below reporting thresholds. 
Layering: Moving money across multiple accounts to hide its origin. 
Circular Money Flow: Repeatedly transferring funds among interconnected accounts. 
Mule Accounts: Using multiple individuals or fake accounts to transfer funds. 
Shared Device/IP Fraud: Operating several accounts from the same device or network. 
Crypto Off-ramping: Moving illicit funds into cryptocurrency wallets. 
Since investigators usually analyze transactions individually using spreadsheets or rule-based reports, identifying hidden transaction networks becomes time-consuming and error-prone.
Financial institutions need an intelligent Anti-Money Laundering (AML) platform that analyzes entire transaction networks instead of isolated transactions, automatically detects suspicious transaction patterns and hidden relationships in real time, explains why accounts are considered high risk, prioritizes investigations using AI-driven risk scores, visualizes complex financial connections, and generates regulator-ready investigation reports to improve the speed, accuracy, and effectiveness of financial crime detection and compliance.
This solution aims to leverage Artificial Intelligence, Graph Databases, and Explainable AI to enable faster, more accurate, and more efficient financial crime investigations while significantly reducing manual effort.
Objective:
Develop an AI-powered web-based Anti-Money Laundering (AML) Investigation Platform that enables banks and financial institutions to automatically detect suspicious financial activities, visualize transaction networks, assign intelligent risk scores, and generate investigation reports using machine learning and graph analytics.
Input:
The application should support multiple methods of importing financial transaction data.
1. Transaction Dataset
Supported formats: CSV, Excel (.xlsx) ,REST API
Sample Demo Dataset attached below
The dataset contains:
100,000+ synthetic banking transactions 
Customer information 
KYC details 
Device information 
IP addresses 
Transaction channels 
Fraud labels 
AML scenarios 
High-risk accounts 
Blacklisted entities
Processing:
After uploading the dataset, the platform automatically performs multiple stages of analysis.
Stage 1 — Data Validation
The system validates incoming data by:
Removing duplicate transactions 
Handling missing values 
Verifying mandatory fields 
Standardizing timestamps 
Validating account numbers
Stage 2 — Data Transformation
The validated data is converted into graph structures.This graph enables detection of hidden transaction relationships

Stage 3 — Graph Analytics
This stage analyzes transaction networks by representing accounts, transactions, devices, beneficiaries, and IP addresses as interconnected entities. The system detects multi-hop transfers, circular transactions, shared beneficiaries, hidden transaction chains, common devices, shared IP addresses, and transaction clusters. By uncovering these hidden relationships, it provides a complete view of suspicious financial networks, enabling investigators to identify complex money laundering patterns and prioritize high-risk cases more effectively.
Stage 4 — AI-Based Risk Analysis
Machine learning models continuously analyze transaction behavior to identify suspicious financial activities such as structuring (smurfing), where large transactions are split into smaller amounts to avoid reporting thresholds; layering, in which funds are transferred through multiple intermediary accounts to conceal their origin; circular money flow, where funds repeatedly circulate among connected accounts; mule account activity, where accounts receive and immediately forward funds; shared device/IP fraud, involving multiple accounts operated from the same device or IP address; blacklisted entity connections, where transactions involve sanctioned or suspicious entities; and crypto off-ramping, where illicit funds are moved into cryptocurrency wallets to obscure their traceability.
Stage 5 — AI Risk Scoring
The platform assigns an AI-driven risk score to each account by evaluating multiple behavioral and network-based factors. The score considers transaction velocity, transaction amount, historical transaction behavior, network centrality, device sharing, IP address similarity, connections with blacklisted entities, and transaction frequency. By combining these indicators, the system prioritizes high-risk accounts, enabling investigators to focus on the most suspicious cases and accelerate the AML investigation process.

Every account receives an AI-generated Risk Score between 0–100.
Example
Score	Risk Level
0–30	Low
31–60	Medium
61–80	High
81–100	Critical
Output:
1. Intelligent Transaction Analysis
The application automatically analyzes uploaded transaction data and classifies each transaction based on its risk level. Using AI and predefined fraud detection models, it identifies suspicious activities such as structuring, layering, circular money flow, mule accounts, shared device/IP usage, and transactions involving blacklisted or high-risk entities.
2.AI-Based Risk Scoring
Every customer and account is assigned a dynamic AI-generated risk score ranging from 0 to 100. The score is calculated based on multiple factors including transaction frequency, transaction amount, network connectivity, device sharing, historical behavior, and suspicious transaction patterns. Along with the score, the system provides an explanation of why the account has been marked as risky.
3. Interactive Transaction Network Visualization
The platform generates an interactive graph showing relationships between accounts, customers, and transactions. Investigators can visually trace the flow of money, explore connected accounts, identify hidden fraud rings, and understand complex transaction networks that are difficult to detect using traditional reports.
4. Real-Time Fraud Alerts
Whenever suspicious activities are detected, the system automatically generates alerts and categorizes them by severity. Compliance officers receive notifications for events such as structuring, layering, rapid transaction spikes, shared devices, blacklisted account interactions, and crypto wallet transfers, enabling quicker investigation and response.
5. AI Investigation Assistant(optional)
The platform includes an AI-powered assistant that allows investigators to ask questions in natural language. Users can request explanations for suspicious transactions, identify connected accounts, summarize investigation findings, or retrieve insights about specific customers without manually analyzing large volumes of data.
6. Automated Investigation Report
After completing the analysis, the application generates a detailed investigation report in PDF format. The report includes suspicious accounts, transaction timelines, AI-generated risk scores, explanations, transaction network visualizations, and recommended actions. This report can be shared with compliance teams and regulatory authorities for further review.
7. Analytics Dashboard
The application provides a centralized dashboard displaying key AML metrics such as total transactions processed, suspicious transactions, high-risk accounts, critical alerts, fraud trends, geographic distribution of suspicious activities, and overall investigation statistics. This enables compliance teams to monitor financial crime risks in real time and prioritize investigations effectively.

