This gives a realistic imbalance where only a small fraction of transactions are suspicious.

Columns in the Dataset
Column	Description
transactionId	Unique transaction ID
timestamp	Date and time of transaction
customerId	Customer identifier
customerName	Synthetic customer name
kycStatus	Verified / Pending
riskProfile	Customer's base risk profile
senderAccount	Sender account number
receiverAccount	Receiver account number
senderCity	Sender location
receiverCity	Receiver location
amountINR	Transaction amount
currency	INR
transactionType	UPI / IMPS / NEFT / RTGS
deviceId	Device identifier
ipAddress	IP address
isBlacklistedReceiver	Yes / No
amlScenario	Fraud pattern
expectedRisk	Ground truth risk
remarks	Description

AI Output
Your application should not directly display the expectedRisk column. That column is only for validating your AI model.
Instead, your AI should generate something like:
Output	Description
Risk Score	0–100
Risk Category	Low / Medium / High / Critical
Suspicious Pattern	Structuring, Layering, etc.
AI Explanation	Why the transaction is suspicious
Alert	Yes / No

Risk Score Logic
For your demo, you can define the following scoring model:
Score	Category	Action
0–30	Low	Normal transaction
31–60	Medium	Monitor
61–80	High	Investigator review
81–100	Critical	Immediate AML alert

